    {
      "id": "4vowryd",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2.2 Physiographic Divisions"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Maharashtra can be divided into four major physiographic regions based on relief, drainage, and climate.",
      "id": "8lglhrw"
    },
    {
      "id": "x9r9l3a",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "1. The Sahyadri Range (Western Ghats)"
    },
    {
      "id": "e6my5kv",
      "type": "image",
      "imageId": "mpe2lx6",
      "src": "/books/Guide/MAHARSHTRA GS/images/image_mpe2lx6.jpeg"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "The Sahyadri mountain range, also called the Western Ghats, runs along the western edge of Maharashtra from north to south for about 800 km. It is the backbone of Maharashtra's geography.",
      "id": "2zgisra"
    },
    {
      "id": "33xo3lt",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "Average height: 1000–1300 metres",
        "Highest peak in Maharashtra: Kalsubai (1646 m) in Ahmednagar district",
        "Other important peaks: Salher (1567 m), Harishchandragad (1424 m), Rajgad",
        "The ghats receive heavy rainfall (3000–5000 mm annually)",
        "Source of all major rivers of Maharashtra",
        "Declared a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2012 (as part of Western Ghats)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "s1okfnl",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Important Ghats (Passes) in Sahyadri"
    },
    {
      "id": "n255j77",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Thal Ghat</strong></p>",
            "<p>Mumbai–Nashik (Kasara) railway line passes through this</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Bhor Ghat</strong></p>",
            "<p>Mumbai–Pune railway and highway pass through this</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Amba Ghat</strong></p>",
            "<p>Ratnagiri–Kolhapur road</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Amboli Ghat</strong></p>",
            "<p>Sindhudurg–Belgaum road</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "2br3pq2",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "2. Konkan Coastal Strip"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "The Konkan region lies between the Sahyadri range and the Arabian Sea. It is a narrow coastal strip running north to south.",
      "id": "hl0k9ao"
    },
    {
      "id": "drtbr7t",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "Districts: Thane, Raigad, Ratnagiri, Sindhudurg (and Mumbai City &amp; Suburban)",
        "Fertile area with major crops: paddy, coconut, mango, cashew, Alphonso mango",
        "Heavy rainfall (2500–3500 mm per year)",
        "Known for scenic beaches, backwaters, and sea-forts",
        "Konkan Railway (built 1990–1998) connects Mumbai to Goa/Mangalore"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "xpx27is",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "3. The Deccan Plateau (Maharashtra Plateau)"
    },
    {
      "id": "uzm66wk",
      "type": "image",
      "imageId": "okwj1fz",
      "src": "/books/Guide/MAHARSHTRA GS/images/image_okwj1fz.jpeg"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "The Deccan Plateau constitutes the largest part of Maharashtra lying east of the Sahyadri. It slopes from west to east.",
      "id": "8jk7kzv"
    },
    {
      "id": "g13nvny",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "Altitude: 300–900 metres",
        "Formed of volcanic Deccan basalt (trap) rock",
        "Divided into Nashik Plateau, Marathwada Plateau, and Vidarbha Plateau",
        "Black cotton soil (Regur) – ideal for cotton cultivation",
        "Semi-arid climate; rainfall 500–1000 mm"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "z6uujkl",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "4. Vidarbha Region"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Vidarbha is the eastern part of Maharashtra, comprising Amravati and Nagpur divisions. It was formerly part of the Central Provinces.",
      "id": "7g9mheh"
    },
    {
      "id": "j8rhoqe",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "Districts: Nagpur, Amravati, Wardha, Yavatmal, Chandrapur, Gadchiroli, Akola, Washim, Buldhana, Gondia, Bhandara",
        "Known for: Cotton and orange cultivation; coal mines (Chandrapur, Yavatmal, Wardha)",
        "Tadoba-Andhari Tiger Reserve: Famous tiger reserve in Chandrapur",
        "Nagpur: Orange City; geographical centre of India; Winter capital of Maharashtra"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "tsmgz0h",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2.3 Rivers of Maharashtra"
    },
